            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>What Happened This Week</h2>
<p>Entering late Q3, we observed a significant increase in Google Ads account suspension cases. According to data from Yiyu Digital Media's consulting services, inquiries about "account suspensions" rose approximately 40% week-over-week, concentrated in three main scenarios:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>New accounts suspended immediately after first top-up</strong>: Missing brand verification or mismatched payment and registration information</li>
<li><strong>Previously normal accounts suddenly restricted</strong>: System detected abnormal traffic patterns or cross-timezone operations</li>
<li><strong>Chain suspensions from cross-account fund transfers</strong>: Parent account bans triggering sub-account restrictions</li>
</ul>
<p>This wave of suspensions directly correlates with Google's recent tightening of invalid traffic policies. For overseas teams, account suspension means not just interrupted campaigns but potentially losing historical campaign data, Pixel tracking, and billing records.</p>
<h2>Three Common Appeal Mistakes</h2>
<p>Among the appeal cases we've handled, over 60% of first-time rejections stem from inadequate material preparation. Here are the high-frequency issues observed this week:</p>
<h3>Mistake 1: Submitting Only "No Violation" Statements</h3>
<p>This is the most common appeal failure reason. Google's review team needs an <strong>evidence chain</strong>, not emotional appeals. Simply stating "I didn't violate any rules" gets flagged as low-priority processing.</p>
<p>The correct approach: Address each specific issue mentioned in the suspension notice with corresponding supporting materials. For example, if flagged for "suspicious payment activity," provide business licenses, bank statements, and company charters that confirm your business legitimacy.</p>
<h3>Mistake 2: Ignoring Hidden Information in the UI</h3>
<p>Many teams only screenshot the campaigns interface when appealing, missing two critical pieces of information:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Account Rating page</strong>: Displays historical violation records and credit scores</li>
<li><strong>Billing transactions page</strong>: Complete top-up records and refund history</li>
</ul>
<p>This information helps reviewers quickly verify account operational status and shortens review cycles.</p>
<h3>Mistake 3: Inconsistent Information Across Multi-Account Appeals</h3>
<p>For teams managing multiple Google Ads accounts, company names, addresses, and contact information in appeal materials must be completely consistent. Recently, a team had their sub-account appeal rejected because the parent and sub-account registration details had minor discrepancies (e.g., "Building" vs. "Building," with an extra comma).</p>
<h2>Appeal Material Checklist</h2>
<p>For the suspension types observed this week, here's the recommended material checklist:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Suspension Reason</th>
<th>Required Materials</th>
<th>Bonus Materials</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Payment Issues</td>
<td>3-month bank statements, corporate account verification</td>
<td>Third-party payment platform statements</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Invalid Traffic</td>
<td>Traffic source analysis report, third-party monitoring data</td>
<td>Attribution model documentation</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Policy Violations</td>
<td>Screenshots of flagged content, remediation explanation</td>
<td>Historical campaign qualification proof</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Identity Verification</td>
<td>Legal representative ID, company registration documents</td>
<td>Brand trademark certificate</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Practical tip</strong>: Submit all documents in English or with English translations. Check PDF clarity before uploading — resolution should be at least 300dpi.</p>
<h2>Recommendations for Next Week</h2>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Act immediately</strong>: If your account is under appeal, this week is the window for supplementary materials. Review teams typically reassess within 48 hours of receiving additional information.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Prevention first</strong>: Teams not yet affected should complete Brand Verification and 2SV (Two-Step Verification) binding this week to reduce triggering risk controls.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Backup plan</strong>: For high-value accounts, consider preparing standby accounts to ensure campaign continuity if the primary account encounters issues.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>Account suspensions are challenging but most cases have resolution paths. The key is appeal materials that are "factual and evidenced" rather than "emotional and plea-based."</p>
